<h1>layered graph</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="connectedGraph.html"><em>connected graph</em></a> where "layers" L<sub>0</sub> &hellip; L<sub>k</sub> <a href="partition.html"><em>partition</em></a> the <a href="vertex.html"><em>vertices</em></a>.  Each <a href="edge.html"><em>edge</em></a>, which has a nonnegative integral <a href="weightedGraph.html"><em>weight</em></a>, connects only vertices in successive layers.  The width is the greatest number of vertices in any layer, i.e.,  MAX<sub>i=0</sub><sup>k</sup> |L<sub>i</sub>|.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="connectedGraph.html"><em>connected graph</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Specialization</strong> (... is a kind of me.)<br>
<a href="bipartgraph.html"><em>bipartite graph</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
A bipartite graph is a layered graph with two layers. </p> 
<p> <strong>A. Fiat, D.P. Foster, H.J. Karloff, Y. Rabani, Y. Ravid, and S. Vishwanathan.</strong> <em>Competitive Algorithms for Layered Graph Traversal.</em> SIAM J. Comput., to appear.  Preliminary version appeared in FOCS '91.  Contributed by Giovanni Fiaschi &lt;<a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="2e694741584f4040470068474f5d4d46476e434f5c4d414047004d4143">[email&#160;protected]</a>&gt; June 2000.</em>
</p>

